ismiss alert\nL114: \nL115:   1. 【61†phosphor-icons】\nL116:   2. 【62†react】\nL117: ## Repository navigation\nL118: \nL119:   *   * 【62†Code】\nL120:   * 【63†Issues15 (15)】\nL121:   * 【64†Pull requests6 (6)】\nL122:   * 【65†Discussions】\nL123:   * 【66†Actions】\nL124:   * 【67†Projects】\nL125:   * 【68†Security and quality】\nL126:   * 【69†Insights】\nL127: \nL128: More items\nL129: \nL130: ## Files Expand file tree\nL131: \nL132: master\nL133: \nL134: ## Breadcrumbs\nL135: \nL136:   1. 【70†react】\nL137: \nL138: /\nL139: \nL140: # README.md\nL141: \nL142: Copy path\nL143: \nL144: [Input: Go to file]\nL145: \nL146: Blame More file actions\nL147: \nL148: Blame More file actions\nL149: \nL150: ## Latest commit\nL151: \nL152: \nL153: \nL154: ## History\nL155: \nL156: History\nL157: \nL158: master\nL159: \nL160: ## Breadcrumbs\nL161: \nL162:   1. 【70†react】\nL163: \nL164: /\nL165: \nL166: # README.md\nL167: \nL168: Copy path\nL169: \nL170: Top\nL171: ## File metadata and controls\nL172: \nL173:   * Preview\nL174: \nL175:   * Code\nL176: \nL177:   * Blame\nL178: \nL179: 270 lines (210 loc) · 15 KB\nL180: \nL181: 【71†Raw】\nL182: \nL183: Copy raw file\nL184: \nL185: Download raw file\nL186: \nL187: Outline\nL188: \nL189: Edit and raw actions\nL190: \nL191: Important\nL192: As part of a major update, we will be replacing the existing `phosphor-react` package with `@phosphor-icons/react`. We recommend using this new version, as it has improved performance and a significantly smaller bundle size. No APIs have been changed, so drop-in replacement should be straightforward. The legacy package will continue to receive maintenance, but will not be updated with new icons upstream. 【72†Take me to the legacy version ➜】\nL193: # @phosphor-icons/react\nL194: \nL195: Phosphor is a flexible icon family for interfaces, diagrams, presentations — whatever, really. Explore all our icons at 【73†phosphoricons.com†phosphoricons.com】.\nL196: \nL197: ## Installation\nL198: \nL199:     npm i @phosphor-icons/react\nL200: ## Usage\nL201: \nL202: Simply import the icons you need, and add them anywhere in your render method. Phosphor supports tree-shaking, so your bundle only includes code for the icons you use.\nL203: \nL204:     import { HorseIcon, HeartIcon, CubeIcon } from \"@phosphor-icons/react\";\nL205: \nL206:     const App = () => {\nL207:       return (\nL208:         <main>\nL209:           <HorseIcon />\nL210:           <HeartIcon color=\"#AE2983\" weight=\"fill\" size={32} />\nL211:           <CubeIcon color=\"teal\" weight=\"duotone\" />\nL212:         </main>\nL213:       );\nL214:     };\nL215: ### Import Performance Optimization\nL216: \nL217: When importing icons during development directly from the main module `@phosphor-icons/react`, some bundlers may eagerly transpile all 9,000+ modules exported by the package. This behavior can drastically increase compilation time. To avoid transpiling all modules, import individual icons from their specific file paths instead:\nL218: \nL219:     import { BellSimpleIcon } from \"@phosphor-icons/react/dist/csr/BellSimple\";\nL220: #### Next.js Specific Optimizations\nL221: \nL222: If you're using Next.js 13+, consider using 【74†optimizePackageImports†nextjs.org】 in your next.config.js to have Next.js only load the modules that you are actually using. With this approach, you can use `@phosphor-icons/react` directly without causing Next.js to compile all its modules:\nL223: \nL224:     module.exports = {\nL225:       experimental: {\nL226:         optimizePackageImports: [\"@phosphor-icons/react\"],\nL227:       },\nL228:     }\nL229: ### React Server Components and SSR\nL230: \nL231: When using Phosphor Icons in an SSR environment, within a React Server Component, or in any environment that does not permit the use of the Context API (【75†Next.js†nextjs.org】 Server Component, for example), import icons from the `/dist/ssr` submodule:\nL232: \nL233:     import { FishIcon } from \"@phosphor-icons/react/ssr\";\nL234: \nL235:     const MyServerComponent = () => {\nL236:       return <FishIcon weight=\"duotone\" />;\nL237:     };\nL238: \nL239: Note\nL240: These variants do not use React Context, and thus cannot inherit styles from an ancestor `IconContext`.\nL241: ### Props\nL242: \nL243: Icon components accept all props that you can pass to a normal SVG element, including inline `style` objects, `onClick` handlers, and more. The main way of styling them will usually be with the following props:\nL244:   * color?: `string` – Icon stroke/fill color. Can be any CSS color string, including `hex`, `rgb`, `rgba`, `hsl`, `hsla`, named colors, or the special `currentColor` variable.\nL245:   * size?: `number | string` – Icon height & width. As with standard React elements, this can be a number, or a string with units in `px`, `%`, `em`, `rem`, `pt`, `cm`, `mm`, `in`.\nL246:   * weight?: `\"thin\" | \"light\" | \"regular\" | \"bold\" | \"fill\" | \"duotone\"` – Icon weight/style. Can also be used, for example, to \"toggle\" an icon's state: a rating component could use Stars with `weight=\"regular\"` to denote an empty star, and `weight=\"fill\"` to denote a filled star.\nL247:   * mirrored?: `boolean` – Flip the icon horizontally. Can be useful in RTL languages where normal icon orientation is not appropriate.\nL248:   * alt?: `string` – Add accessible alt text to an icon.\nL249: ### Context\nL250: Phosphor takes advantage of React Context to make applying a default style to all icons simple. Create an `IconContext.Provider` at the root of the app (or anywhere above the icons in the tree) and pass in a configuration object with props to be applied by default to all icons:\nL251: \nL252:     import { IconContext, HorseIcon, HeartIcon, CubeIcon } from \"@phosphor-icons/react\";\nL253: \nL254:     const App = () => {\nL255:       return (\nL256:         <IconContext.Provider\nL257:           value={{\nL258:             color: \"limegreen\",\nL259:             size: 32,\nL260:             weight: \"bold\",\nL261:             mirrored: false,\nL262:           }}\nL263:         >\nL264:           <div>\nL265:             <HorseIcon /> {/* I'm lime-green, 32px, and bold! */}\nL266:             <HeartIcon /> {/* Me too! */}\nL267:             <CubeIcon /> {/* Me three :) */}\nL268:           </div>\nL269:         </IconContext.Provider>\nL270:       );\nL271:     };\nL272: You may create multiple Contexts for styling icons differently in separate regions of an application; icons use the nearest Context above them to determine their style.\nL273: \nL274: Note\nL275: \nL276: The context will also pass any provided SVG props down to icon instances, which can be useful E.G. in adding accessible `aria-label`s, `classNames`, etc.\nL277: \nL278: Note\nL279: \nL280: React Context is not available in some environments. See 【76†React Server Components and SSR】 for details.\nL281: ### Composability\nL282: \nL283: Components can accept arbitrary SVG elements as children, so long as they are valid children of the `<svg>` element. This can be used to modify an icon with background layers or shapes, filters, animations, and more. The children will be placed below the normal icon contents.\nL284: The following will cause the Cube icon to rotate and pulse:\nL285: \nL286:     const RotatingCube = () => {\nL287:       return (\nL288:         <CubeIcon color=\"darkorchid\" weight=\"duotone\">\nL289:           <animate\nL290:             attributeName=\"opacity\"\nL291:             values=\"0;1;0\"\nL292:             dur=\"4s\"\nL293:             repeatCount=\"indefinite\"\nL294:           ></animate>\nL295:           <animateTransform\nL296:             attributeName=\"transform\"\nL297:             attributeType=\"XML\"\nL298:             type=\"rotate\"\nL299:             dur=\"5s\"\nL300:             from=\"0 0 0\"\nL301:             to=\"360 0 0\"\nL302:             repeatCount=\"indefinite\"\nL303:           ></animateTransform>\nL304:         </CubeIcon>\nL305:       );\nL306:     };\nL307: Note\nL308: \nL309: The coordinate space of slotted elements is relative to the contents of the icon `viewBox`, which is 256x256 square. Only 【77†valid SVG elements†developer.mozilla.org】 will be rendered.\nL310: ### Imports\nL311: \nL312: You may wish to import all icons at once for use in your project, though depending on your bundler this could prevent tree-shaking and make your app's bundle larger.\nL313: \nL314:     import * as Icon from \"@phosphor-icons/react\";\nL315: \nL316:     <Icon.SmileyIcon />\nL317:     <Icon.FolderIcon weight=\"thin\" />\nL318:     <Icon.BatteryHalfIcon size=\"24px\" />\nL319: \nL320: For information on using Phosphor Icons in Server Components, see See 【76†React Server Components and SSR】.\nL321: ### Custom Icons\nL322: \nL323: It is possible to extend Phosphor with your custom icons, taking advantage of the styling and context abstractions used in our library. To create a custom icon, first design your icons on a 256x256 pixel grid, and export them as SVG. For best results, flatten the icon so that you only export assets with `path` elements. Strip any `fill` or `stroke` attributes, as these will be inherited from the wrapper.\nL324: Next, create a new React `forwardRef` component, importing the `IconBase` component, as well as the `Icon` and `IconWeight` types from this library. Define a `Map<IconWeight, ReactElement>` that maps each icon weight to the contents of each SVG asset, effectively removing the wrapping `<svg>` element from each. Name your component, and render an `<IconBase />`, passing all props and the ref, as well as the `weights` you defined earlier, as JSX props:\nL325:     import { forwardRef, ReactElement } from \"react\";\nL326:     import { Icon, IconBase, IconWeight } from \"@phosphor-icons/react\";\nL327: \nL328:     const weights = new Map<IconWeight, ReactElement>([\nL329:       [\"thin\", <path d=\"...\" />],\nL330:       [\"light\", <path d=\"...\" />],\nL331:       [\"regular\", <path d=\"...\" />],\nL332:       [\"bold\", <path d=\"...\" />],\nL333:       [\"fill\", <path d=\"...\" />],\nL334:       [\nL335:         \"duotone\",\nL336:         <>\nL337:           <path d=\"...\" opacity=\"0.2\" />\nL338:           <path d=\"...\" />\nL339:         </>,\nL340:       ],\nL341:     ]);\nL342:     const CustomIcon: Icon = forwardRef((props, ref) => (\nL343:       <IconBase ref={ref} {...props} weights={weights} />\nL344:     ));\nL345: \nL346:     CustomIcon.displayName = \"CustomIcon\";\nL347: \nL348:     export default CustomIcon;\nL349: Note\nL350: \nL351: If you have multiple child elements, wrap them in a `Fragment`. Typically our `duotone` icons have multiple elements, with the background layer at 20% opacity.\nL352: \nL353: If Custom Icons are intended to be used in 【76†React Server Components】, use `<SSRBase />` instead of `<IconBase />` as the render component.\nL354: ## Our Projects\nL355:   * 【78†@phosphor-icons/homepage】 ▲ Phosphor homepage and general info\nL356:   * 【79†@phosphor-icons/core】 ▲ Phosphor icon assets and catalog\nL357:   * 【80†@phosphor-icons/elm】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Elm\nL358:   * 【81†@phosphor-icons/figma】 ▲ Phosphor icons Figma plugin\nL359:   * 【82†@phosphor-icons/flutter】 ▲ Phosphor IconData library for Flutter\nL360:   * 【83†@phosphor-icons/pack】 ▲ Phosphor web font stripper to generate minimal icon bundles\nL361:   * 【84†@phosphor-icons/penpot】 ▲ Phosphor icons Penpot plugin\nL362:   * 【62†@phosphor-icons/react】 ▲ Phosphor icon component library for React\nL363:   * 【85†@phosphor-icons/sketch】 ▲ Phosphor icons Sketch plugin\nL364:   * 【86†@phosphor-icons/swift】 ▲ Phosphor icon component library for SwiftUI\nL365:   * 【87†@phosphor-icons/theme】 ▲ A VS Code (and other IDE) theme with the Phosphor color palette\nL366:   * 【88†@phosphor-icons/unplugin】 ▲ A multi-framework bundler plugin for generating Phosphor sprite sheets\nL367:   * 【89†@phosphor-icons/vue】 ▲ Phosphor icon component library for Vue\nL368:   * 【90†@phosphor-icons/web】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Vanilla JS\nL369:   * 【91†@phosphor-icons/webcomponents】 ▲ Phosphor icons as Web Components\nL370: ## Community Projects\nL371:   * 【92†adamglin0/compose-phosphor-icons】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Compose Multiplatform\nL372:   * 【93†altdsoy/phosphor_icons】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Phoenix and TailwindCSS\nL373:   * 【94†amPerl/egui-phosphor】 ▲ Phosphor icons for egui apps (Rust)\nL374:   * 【95†babakfp/phosphor-icons-svelte】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Svelte apps\nL375:   * 【96†brettkolodny/phosphor-lustre】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Lustre\nL376:   * 【97†cellularmitosis/phosphor-uikit】 ▲ XCode asset catalog generator for Phosphor icons (Swift/UIKit)\nL377:   * 【98†cjohansen/phosphor-clj】 ▲ Phosphor icons as Hiccup for Clojure and ClojureScript\nL378:   * 【99†codeat3/blade-phosphor-icons】 ▲ Phosphor icons in your Laravel Blade views\nL379:   * 【100†dennym/phosphor_icons_ex】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Elixir, Phoenix and Ash\nL380:   * 【101†dreamRs/phosphor-r】 ▲ Phosphor icon wrapper for R documents and applications\nL381:   * 【102†duongdev/phosphor-react-native】 ▲ Phosphor icon component library for React Native\nL382:   * 【103†haruaki07/phosphor-svelte】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Svelte apps\nL383:   * 【104†IgnaceMaes/ember-phosphor-icons】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Ember apps\nL384:   * 【105†iota-uz/icons】 ▲ Phosphor icons as Templ components (Go)\nL385:   * 【106†jajuma/phosphorhyva】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Magento 2 & Mage-OS with Hyvä Theme\nL386:   * 【107†Kitten†kitten.small-web.org】 ▲ Phosphor icons integrated by default in Kitten\nL387:   * 【108†lucagoslar/phosphor-css】 ▲ CSS wrapper for Phosphor SVG icons\nL388:   * 【109†maful/ruby-phosphor-icons】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Ruby and Rails applications\nL389:   * 【110†meadowsys/phosphor-svgs】 ▲ Phosphor icons as Rust string constants\nL390:   * 【111†mwood/tamagui-phosphor-icons】 ▲ Phosphor icons for Tamagui\nL391:   * 【112†noozo/phosphoricons_elixir】 ▲ Phosphor icons as SVG strings for Elixir/Phoenix\nL392:   * 【113†